parts.solution:
The graph of $y = \dfrac{1}{f(x)}$ has:
vertical asymptote $x = -3$;
horizontal asymptotes $y = 0$ and $y = \dfrac{1}{2}$;
$y$-intercept $\left(0, -\dfrac{2}{3}\right)$;
maximum point $(5, 1)$.

subparts.solution:
For the reciprocal graph:
the maximum point $(-2, 3)$ becomes a minimum point $\left(-2, \frac{1}{3}\right)$;
the $x$-intercept $(-1.5, 0)$ becomes a vertical asymptote $x = -1.5$;
the vertical asymptotes $x = -1$ and $x = 1$ become $x$-intercepts $(-1, 0)$ and $(1, 0)$;
the stationary point of inflexion $(0, 0)$ becomes a vertical asymptote $x = 0$.
